WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.160
What if one ai could keep the exact same character and dog looking perfect through an entire short film? This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 168. You know that feeling when a character in a video suddenly looks like a different person, and the whole story wobbles? A filmmaker needed one man and one dog to show up in every scene of a short film, looking like themselves the whole way through. The tools they picked were Cling 3.0, which makes video,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:35.580
video, and Astra 6, which makes still images. Together those two artificial intelligence tools

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:41.660
or AI tools, built the scenes while holding the character's steady. AI here just means

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:47.040
software that has practiced on patterns in pictures and video until it can make new ones.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:59.140
The finished clip went up on Instagram and people on Reddit started passing it around. Watch

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.540
Picture trying to sketch the same person from memory 20 times in a row.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.440
Most of us would nudge the nose, change the jacket, or forget a spot on the dog.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.540
These tools are getting better at pinning down those exact details instead of guessing

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.000
a new version each time.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:21.640
That matters because a YouTube series, a TikTok story, a GameCut scene, or a school project

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.280
falls apart when the hero morphs by accident.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.540
Fixing every frame by hand is like editing a stop-motion film with tweezers.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.040
It can eat hours.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.780
For anyone making videos or trying animation as a hobby, this kind of lock on a face

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.220
could let one person tell a polished story without a whole crew.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.800
It also opens a door for teens who want to mess around with short films, and maybe chase

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.060
that as a career later.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.460
The experiment shows current video and image tools can carry a longer sequence without

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.440
the old problem of details slowly wandering off. Think of that wander like a

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.220
photocopy of a photocopy. Each copy gets a little blurrier until the character

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.160
is barely the same. Open Instagram and look for the reel of that man and dog.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.640
Pause every few seconds and check the face, the outfit, and the fur. The creator

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.060
also name the two tools, so you can try a similar experiment if you want to build

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.860
your own tiny cast. Let me walk through how that visual memory actually holds

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.460
because this is the part that makes the trick click.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.480
Start with a school play.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.620
The same student plays the same role in every scene,

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.480
so the costume, the wig, and the face paint

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.880
have to match from the cafeteria to the gym.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.140
If wardrobe swaps the jacket halfway through,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.620
the audience thinks it is a new character, the plot trips.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.780
Creators now do a digital version of that costume Bible.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.600
They lock the look first,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.860
like taping a character sketch to the wall.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:49.960
All.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.580
Astros 6 handles the still pictures.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.580
The way a reference drawing shows the face shape, the clothes, and the dog's fur pattern

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.180
before anyone yells action.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:06.000
Kling 3.0 then turns matched looks into moving scenes, like flipping a notebook of drawings

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.580
that all show the same pair.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.940
Step 1 is freeze the design.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.440
Step 2 is check every new shot against that design.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.840
Step 3 is string those shots into video without letting the face drift.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.060
A Minecraft skin is a useful picture here.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.140
Your character keeps the same pixels while you run across a whole world.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.780
Older video tools were more like redrawing the skin from scratch for every biome, so

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.140
the haircut kept mutating.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.360
The newer pairing is closer to moving one sticker onto new backgrounds.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.640
The sticker is the man and the dog.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.360
The backgrounds are the scenes.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.400
Once you see it that way, a consistent character is not magic.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.900
It is a stubborn memory for little details that used to slip.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.620
You already understand this if you have ever kept an original character looking the same

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.400
across a comic, a game, or a TikTok series.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:01.740
A company called Agility built a humanoid robot that can freeze and even squat if it senses

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.200
a person getting too close.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.720
Humanoid just means it is shaped a bit like us, with a body and legs, instead of a box

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:09.260
on wheels.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.700
skills. Older factory robots often lived inside cages, because they could not tell when

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.020
a human might get bumped. That is like parking a lawnmower behind a fence because it cannot

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:25.740
see your feet. The new design lets the robot share a room with people and skip the extra

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:31.720
barriers. The squat is the part you can actually see. The machine changes its own behavior to

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:37.560
avoid contact, instead of hoping a wall will do the job. It is a bit like a careful classmate

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:43.460
who drops low in a crowded hallway so nobody collides. Places this could matter later include

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:50.300
warehouses, homes, or schools. Anywhere a robot might help carry things. If robots that help

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:55.120
with chores or deliveries interest you, this is how safety gets built in from the start,

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:01.340
not bolted on after a scare. Search R's Technica for agility's humanoid robot,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:06.260
Watch the demo and look specifically for that squat when a person steps near.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:12.880
Notice how it stops first, then changes height, instead of rolling forward like an old caged arm.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.440
About 20% of people in Britain already ask AI chatbots for news updates.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:24.360
Tools like Chat GPT, a chatbot you type questions into, are part of that shift.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.940
Most of those people still do not fully trust the answers, which is actually a healthy habit.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:35.960
chat bots are sliding from homework helpers into everyday information sources. The way a search

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:41.480
bar once did. Treat the answers like a very confident classmate who did not show their notes.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:46.900
Smooth sentences are not the same thing as proof. If you want a tiny experiment,

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:53.100
open chat GPT and type this. Give me three news headlines from today in plain language.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:59.020
Then open a news app you already use and compare. See what matches, what is missing,

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.040
and what feels too sure of itself.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.660
That double check is the whole point.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.200
Curiosity plus a second source beats blind trust.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.080
Open AI is working with Anthropic

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.260
and Google DeepMind on tests meant

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.600
to keep powerful models from causing problems.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.220
Models in this case are the actual programs

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.120
that chat, write, and make pictures.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.760
The idea is to share safety methods across companies,

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.760
even while those companies still compete on features.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.880
pictures. Picture rival sports teams that still share concussion rules, because some

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:37.580
risks are bigger than one locker room. Cooperation on safety testing is one way the field is

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:43.160
trying to handle trouble that no single lab can cover alone. And that's a wrap. If any

